  image("/results/figures/concept.svg", width: 88%),
  caption: [Differential in one picture. Offline, a circuit model and its fault catalog are simulated under part tolerances with SPICE (standard circuit-simulation software), and the results are learned as fault signatures. Online, a Bayesian loop picks each measurement while the language model (LLM) only reads and explains. Safety rules and the numeric grounding check run in code beneath both. Terms are explained in Appendix C.],
) <fig:concept>

<< part(1, "Problem, stakeholders and industry context") >>
<% set cs = m('library.circuits.channel_strip') %>
<% set hv = m('hv_audit.circuits.channel_strip') %>

*Finding the fault is a separately billed step.* Three specialist shops we sampled (studio, vintage hi-fi and tube amplifier) charge \$125 to \$150 for a bench evaluation; one fee covers the first hour of work and another is credited to the repair << cite('rates_studio_doctors', 'rates_archer_hifi', 'rates_cw_guitar_audio') >>. A shop servicing studio outboard gear bills its one-hour minimum even when no fault is found << cite('rates_british_audio') >>, and vintage-audio repair queues stretch to months or years as specialists reach retirement age << cite('headphonesty_vintage_techs_2026') >>. These are single-shop examples; the share of a repair bill that diagnosis takes is not published, so we will ask working technicians.

*A small workforce, and trainees who need support.* The closest U.S. occupation, audiovisual equipment installers and repairers, employed 24,720 people in May 2023 << cite('bls_oews_492097_2023') >>, with faster-than-average growth projected << cite('onet_492097_bright_outlook') >>. The broader group of electrical and electronics installers and repairers (about 117,700 jobs in 2025) is mostly industrial, avionics and vehicle work << cite('bls_ooh_eeir') >>; it is the adjacent market for the same method. Trade reporting says analog circuitry is not taught at two-year colleges << cite('stereophile_retales_13') >>. In a field study of 5,179 customer-support agents, an AI assistant raised productivity by 14% on average and by 34% for novice and low-skilled workers << cite('brynjolfsson_genai_at_work_2023') >>, so trainees and newly qualified technicians are the users likely to gain most. The equipment is old and wears in known ways: electrolytic capacitors dry out, losing capacitance and gaining resistance << cite('siegen_pedc_lecture08_capacitors') >>, and tube supply has been strained since early 2022 << cite('headphonesty_tube_shortage_2025') >>, so replacing good parts on a guess wastes scarce parts.

*Where current AI stands.* Maintenance AI serves instrumented fleets and common devices: PTC's ServiceMax AI grounds answers in each asset's documented history << cite('ptc_servicemax_ai_2025') >>, Microsoft's field-service Copilot searches product manuals << cite('msft_d365_field_service_page') >>, Siemens pairs repair guidance with predictive maintenance << cite('siemens_maintenance_copilot_2025') >>, IBM's Maximo assistant plans multi-step maintenance tasks << cite('ibm_mas_92_2026') >>, and iFixit's FixBot draws on 125,000 repair guides << cite('ifixit_fixbot_2025') >>. None models the circuit of a unit without sensors (@tab:stakeholders lists who else is affected). Language models are now strong at textbook circuit analysis: the top model on the September 2026 Razavi-Bench scored 95.50% << cite('razavi_bench_2026') >>. A unit on the bench is a different problem: parts sit anywhere within tolerance, different faults can give the same readings, measurements cost effort, and some points carry lethal voltage. One system reached 97.59% on undergraduate circuit problems only after adding a simulation loop that flags mismatches << cite('e2e_circuit_analysis_2025') >>; Differential is built around that kind of check.

